# oasdiff breaking-change ALLOWLIST — deliberate breaks only.
#
# Passed to `oasdiff breaking` via --err-ignore. Any breaking change NOT listed here fails
# CI. Listing one is an explicit, reviewed act: a break must be CHOSEN, not discovered by a
# client.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT — verified against oasdiff v1.28.0. Do not guess at this; the obvious
# guess is wrong and fails silently.
#
# One line per (METHOD, PATH, MESSAGE) triple, all lowercased. oasdiff suppresses a
# breaking change only when an ignore line contains ALL THREE of the method, the path,
# and the change text.
#
#   A line carrying the printed message ALONE matches nothing. It does not error —
#   it silently fails to suppress, and the gate stays red with no explanation.
#
# To derive a line, run the diff and read the two indented lines of the error:
#
#   error   [response-required-property-removed] at openapi.yaml
#           in API GET /widgets/{id}                                      <- method + path
#             removed the required property `displayName` from the ...    <- message
#
# Concatenate them and lowercase the result:
#
#   in api get /widgets/{id} removed the required property `displayname` from the ...
#
# A break affecting two operations needs TWO lines — one per operation.
#
# A WORKING EXAMPLE, exercised against real fixtures, lives at
# contract-fixtures/oasdiff-allowlist.txt. Copy its shape, not oasdiff's output.
#
# EVERY ENTRY MUST CARRY: the FS or ADR that authorised the break, and why.
# An entry with no reason is a bug, not an allowance.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# START EMPTY. Do not pre-populate.
#
# It is tempting to add the breaks you expect the first serialization to cause. Don't —
# for two reasons:
#
#   1. An allowlist entry that matches nothing looks like coverage and silently rots.
#      Add an entry only when a real diff has produced a real error message.
#
#   2. Under serialize-on-touch, the FIRST serialization's breaks are invisible to this
#      gate anyway. oasdiff compares the schema document against its OWN previous
#      revision; an endpoint's first serialization has none, and its breaks are relative
#      to whatever described it before — a different document oasdiff never sees. Those
#      are CROSS-DOCUMENT breaks and plane-1 detection is structurally blind to them.
#
# Expect `SKIPPED: no baseline` until the spec first lands on the base branch. That is
# correct, not broken. The ratchet guards every revision AFTER that point.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FS-83TJK (I-F9R7Q-16) — signup became synchronous.
#
# WHY: signup published a member.create command over the broker and answered 202
# before the database was touched, so a duplicate email could never be reported —
# the unique-constraint violation happened inside a consumer that acked and
# discarded it. The client polled check-email for 15 seconds and then reported
# slowness. Signup now calls CreateMember over gRPC and answers 201 with the
# member, which makes the already-built 409 ALREADY_EXISTS path reachable.
#
# Dropping 202 is the deliberate break FS-83TJK §API surface authorises. The 201
# and the 409 are additions and are NOT breaking, so they need no entry.
#
# Line copied verbatim from `make openapi-breaking` output, lowercased.
in api post /api/member/signup removed the success response with the status `202`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FS-83TJK (I-F9R7Q-17) — check-email removed.
#
# WHY: GET /api/member/check-email existed for exactly one reason — signup used
# to answer 202 without creating the account, so the register page polled this
# endpoint once a second, up to fifteen times, to learn when the member showed
# up. I-F9R7Q-16 made signup synchronous and answer 409 ALREADY_EXISTS directly, so
# the poll was deleted and this endpoint lost its only caller and its stated
# purpose in the same change.
#
# Removed WITHOUT a deprecation window deliberately: the only consumer is this
# repo's own generated client, cut over in the same commit, and the endpoint's
# behaviour is actively misleading to build on — CheckEmailExists reports
# Exists:false on ANY error, so during a database outage it reports every email
# as available. Deprecating it would have meant advertising that for a release.
#
# auth-service's gRPC CheckEmailExists is NOT removed; it is recorded as
# callerless in that service's SPECIFICATION.md. Only the HTTP exposure goes.
#
# Line copied verbatim from `make openapi-breaking` output, lowercased.
in api get /api/member/check-email api path removed without deprecation

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FS-18Z5F R4 (I-18Z5F-5): item status gains IN_RUN.
#
# WHY: an item checked out into a run or field world is IN_RUN until the run
# settles, and the instance list returns those rows as they are, so the hub can
# dim them. oasdiff warns on any enum value added to a response. The only
# consumer is this repo's generated client, regenerated in the same change, and
# FS-18Z5F §H teaches the client to render the new value.
#
# Line copied verbatim from `make openapi-breaking` output, lowercased.
in api get /api/items/instances added the new `in_run` enum value to the `result/items/items/status` response property for the response status `200`

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FS-RG1B4 (I-RG1B4-2) — subscriptions removed; no player-facing payment API.
#
# WHY: the game stopped selling subscriptions. The five /api/payment operations
# were the subscription checkout's backend (customer, product setup, subscribe,
# list, entitlement poll), and their only consumer, this repo's /subscription
# page, was deleted in I-RG1B4-1. Leaving them up would keep an authenticated
# route that drives Stripe calls for a product nobody can see.
#
# Removed WITHOUT a deprecation window deliberately: the only consumer is this
# repo's own generated client, regenerated in the same change. payment-service,
# its gRPC surface and POST /webhook/stripe stay, so a later founders package
# builds on them. Only the HTTP exposure goes.
#
# Lines copied verbatim from `make openapi-breaking` output, lowercased.
in api post /api/payment/customer api path removed without deprecation
in api post /api/payment/subscribe api path removed without deprecation
in api get /api/payment/subscription/permission api path removed without deprecation
in api post /api/payment/subscription/setup api path removed without deprecation
in api get /api/payment/subscriptions/{customerid} api path removed without deprecation
